### Step 1: Calculate the maximum zener current

The power rating of the zener diode is \(P_{t} = 1.6 \text{ W}\) and its breakdown voltage is \(V_{t} = 8 \text{ V}\). The maximum current that can safely pass through the zener diode (also known as maximum zener current, \(I_{z\_max}\) or \(I_{t}\)) is given by:
I_{t} = \frac{P_{t}}{V_{t}}
I_{t} = \frac{1.6}{8} = 0.2 \text{ A}

### Step 2: Determine the voltage drop across the series resistance \(R_{s}\)

For safe operation, the series resistance \(R_{s}\) must limit the current when the input voltage is at its maximum. The maximum input voltage is \(10 \text{ V}\) and the zener voltage is \(8 \text{ V}\). Therefore, the voltage drop across \(R_{s}\) is:
V_{R_{s}} = V_{in\_max} - V_{z}
V_{R_{s}} = 10 - 8 = 2 \text{ V}

### Step 3: Calculate the value of \(R_{s}\)

The resistance \(R_{s}\) is calculated using Ohm's law, considering the maximum voltage drop across it and the maximum safe current through the zener diode (assuming the load current is zero or negligible for this calculation, which ensures the zener handles the maximum possible current).
R_{s} = \frac{V_{R_{s}}}{I_{t}}
R_{s} = \frac{2}{0.2} = 10 \Omega
The value of resistance \(R_{s}\) for safe operation of the diode will be \(10 \Omega\).

